# Where each setting comes from, and whether a deployed environment needs it.
#
# Read by local/scripts/doppler-check.sh and doppler-bootstrap.sh. It exists
# because the two tools disagreed about the answer: the checker classified
# settings from a hardcoded list and would demand a MinIO root password in
# production Doppler for a product project -- MinIO runs only in the local
# Docker stack. A checker that asks for things that must never exist is a
# checker people switch off.
#
#
# Format: NAME<space>CLASS[<space>SOURCE[<space>TYPE]]
#
# TYPE is optional and does two things at once: it says what shape the value
# must have, and it is the ONLY permission to read that value at all.
# `config-typecheck.sh` validates typed settings before a deployment migrates
# anything, and refuses to fetch anything without a type -- so a secret is
# never read by declining to type it, which is also the default.
#
# Where a setting has a TYPE but no SOURCE, the SOURCE column holds `-`. The
# column is positional and two other tools read it.
#
#   bool           true or false, and nothing else. Not "truefalse", which is
#                  what two concatenated values produce and what crash-looped
#                  a worker after its migration had already applied.
#   int            digits, optionally signed.
#   url            has a scheme and a host.
#   enum:a|b|c     one of the listed values.
#   nonempty       any value, but present and not blank.
#
#   local        Never in Doppler. Describes the local Docker stack, or is set
#                per Doppler config rather than stored in it.
#   derived      Read from Terraform. SOURCE says how, and is required:
#                  out:<output>   a `terraform output` name. A map keyed by
#                                 environment is indexed automatically.
#                  const:<value>  the same literal in every environment.
#                  self:environment
#                                 the environment's own name.
#   supplied     Nobody can derive it. Prompted, per environment, and a
#                deployment is refused without it.
#   optional     May be set, never demanded. Prompted like supplied, but an
#                empty answer is an answer and `doppler-check` does not fail on
#                its absence.
#
#                For a setting the code is built to do without: the three
#                OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_* names until a collector exists, and the
#                Control Plane pair until there is a Control Plane -- which is
#                the documented bootstrap order (R-001) rather than a gap.
#
#                Without this class the only way to express "may be absent" was
#                to leave a setting undeclared, which is worse: nothing prompts
#                for it, nothing checks it, and the code silently keeps its
#                default forever.
#
# The SOURCE column exists because the first version assumed the setting name
# and the output name were the same. They are not -- ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID comes
# from an output called zitadel_project_ids -- so every derived value silently
# fell through to a prompt and the feature did nothing. A dry run could not show
# that; it only appeared when the lookup was tried against real outputs.

# -- Local stack only --------------------------------------------------------
NODE_ENV local
SMTP_HOST local
SMTP_PORT local - int
SMTP_SECURE local
SMTP_FROM local

# -- Derived from Terraform ---------------------------------------------------
# Not from Terraform, but derived all the same: the environment's own name. It
# has no default in code on purpose -- a missing or misspelled value must stop a
# process rather than silently select an estate -- so a deployed service that
# does not receive it cannot start at all.
ENVIRONMENT derived self:environment enum:dev|test|stg|prod
ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID derived out:zitadel_project_ids
ZITADEL_CLIENT_ID derived out:zitadel_client_ids
REDIS_URL derived out:redis_urls
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME derived const:{{projectSlug}}
NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_URL derived out:admin_urls
NEXT_PUBLIC_PORTAL_URL derived out:portal_urls
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL derived out:api_urls
ZITADEL_DOMAIN derived out:zitadel_domains
ZITADEL_ADMIN_REDIRECT_URI derived out:admin_redirect_uris
ZITADEL_PORTAL_REDIRECT_URI derived out:portal_redirect_uris
CORS_ORIGINS derived out:cors_origins

# -- Supplied by an operator --------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL carries the Supabase password, which Terraform never outputs --
# it is an input, and the module ignores changes to it so it can be rotated
# outside Terraform.
DATABASE_URL supplied
# The privileged credential migrations run as, and the only place it is used.
#
# DATABASE_URL above is the restricted role every service connects as -- it
# cannot create tables, so it cannot migrate. See local/scripts/create-app-role.sh
# and R-032.
#
# Classified here although no service reads it. This file answers "does a
# deployed environment need it", not "does an application read it", and leaving
# it out meant doppler-check.sh never asked for it -- so a missing value
# surfaced in the middle of a deploy, at the migrate step, instead of in the
# preflight that exists to catch exactly this. It was omitted on that reasoning
# and a reader found the gap by reading Doppler and not finding the secret.
DATABASE_ADMIN_URL supplied
# ZITADEL generates this and returns it into state. With nowhere else to put it,
# state becomes its only home -- which is how a committed plan file became a
# copy of the only copy. Doppler is where it belongs.
ZITADEL_CLIENT_SECRET supplied
# The credential the Control Plane acts as when it creates a tenant's ZITADEL
# organization, project and application. That is the Control Plane's job, so
# unlike the client secret above -- which authenticates an end user's sign-in --
# this one authenticates the platform to ZITADEL with no user present.
#
# Locally it is not a Doppler setting at all: the container mints a machine user
# on first boot and writes its personal access token to
# local/zitadel/machinekey/pat, which provision.py reads straight off disk. It is
# classified `supplied` because a *deployed* environment has no such file, and
# there it must belong to a service account created for the purpose -- never the
# one Terraform runs as. A credential that can create organizations should not
# also be able to delete the infrastructure they live in.
ZITADEL_SERVICE_TOKEN supplied
SESSION_SECRET supplied
# Empty is a valid answer and the right one until a collector exists: the
# tracing setup treats an empty endpoint as "no exporter", so spans are still
# created and trace context still propagates across the queue -- only the export
# is off.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT supplied
# Authentication for a hosted collector, as OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS expects
# it: `authorization=Basic <base64>`. Nothing in this codebase reads it -- the
# OpenTelemetry SDK picks it up from the environment on its own, which is why
# Grafana Cloud and the like need no code change. Empty when self-hosting or
# when there is no collector.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS supplied
# Which transport the endpoint speaks: grpc, or http/protobuf for a managed
# collector. Not inferable -- the local collector is an http:// URL that speaks
# gRPC -- and getting it wrong gives a connection error against a healthy
# gateway. The SDK ignores this variable when the exporter class is chosen in
# code, so it is read explicitly by setup_telemetry in core/observability.py.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL supplied

# -- Tunables ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by a service, sensible without being set, and until now impossible to set
# at all: absent from this file meant nothing prompted for them and nothing
# would accept them.
DATABASE_POOL_SIZE optional
# Whether X-Forwarded-For can be believed. Off by default because the header is
# set by anyone who wants to set it, and trusting it where no proxy rewrites it
# hands a caller a fresh rate-limit quota per request. Genuinely per
# environment: on behind a CDN, off in front of one.
TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR optional
